      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new word: what did jalam name, and what did pānīyam name?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ओदनः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ओदनः (odanaḥ) — \"cooked rice\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "अन्नम् (annam) was food in general — \"the eaten thing\". ओदनः (odanaḥ) is narrower and warmer: the grain after it has met the water. Cooked rice, on a plate, now."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The traditional derivation takes it from a root meaning \"to wet\", which reads the word as grain that has been moistened — a description of cooking rather than of rice. Its ending -अः puts it in the masculine family beside मार्गः and ग्रामः, so ओदनः (odanaḥ) and अन्नम् sit in different families while naming the same meal."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A general word for food, and a particular thing on the plate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
