              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new word: what did phalam mean — both of its meanings?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: वस्त्रम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "वस्त्रम् (vastram) — \"cloth, a garment\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You know वसामि (vasāmi), \"I dwell\", from the root vas. वस्त्रम् (vastram) looks like it belongs to that root and does not. Sanskrit has two roots spelled vas: one for dwelling, one for wearing. वसामि belongs to the first, वस्त्रम् (vastram) to the second, and no amount of staring at the letters will separate them — only the meanings do."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The wearing one travelled. Latin vestis, and through it English vest, vestment, invest — which originally meant to clothe someone in the robes of an office. Neuter -अम्."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A word that warns you: same letters, different root."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
